1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,904
This horror demo shipped with Python inside it.
And a machine learning runtime.

2
00:00:05,154 --> 00:00:08,151
In a game about noticing
when something has changed.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,416
Let's do the game first,
because this one is worth explaining.

4
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:24,384
COMPARTMENT 666 is an anomaly hunt. You wake up on a train with no memory of boarding it,
and the only way forward is through the carriages.

5
00:00:24,634 --> 00:00:31,915
You walk into a compartment. You look at it.
Then you decide one thing: is this normal, or has something changed.

6
00:00:32,165 --> 00:00:39,760
Say normal when it's normal, and the train moves on.
Say it wrong, either way, and you lose a life. You get three.

7
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:49,616
If you've played Exit 8, you know the shape of this. It's the loop that game turned into a genre:
same room, again and again, and the only skill is noticing.

8
00:00:49,866 --> 00:00:57,696
What this one adds is a radio. A little handheld device that picks up anomalies you can't see,
and you don't get to use it forever.

9
00:01:03,946 --> 00:01:14,388
The dressing is an early 1900s express. Red velvet, oil paintings, brass.
Which is a smart choice, because a period carriage is full of objects you don't have a memory for.

10
00:01:14,638 --> 00:01:21,214
And the anomalies are graded easy, medium and hard,
with a different death animation waiting when you get one wrong.

11
00:01:21,464 --> 00:01:29,529
It went up on Steam today. Single player, fifteen languages,
no reviews yet. Which means the only thing I can actually check is the build.

12
00:01:29,779 --> 00:01:34,161
So. One point four eight gigabytes,
thirty six files.

13
00:01:34,411 --> 00:01:41,719
Unreal Engine, and almost all of it is one container:
one point one six gigabytes in a single ucas.

14
00:01:41,969 --> 00:01:46,090
Then I looked at the executable,
and it isn't called what you'd expect.

15
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:56,861
The launcher is COMPARTMENT_666 dot exe. But the actual binary,
the hundred and forty megabyte one, is called UnrealGame-Win64-Shipping.

16
00:01:57,111 --> 00:02:03,034
That's Unreal's default.
Every other Unreal demo I've opened renamed it. This project never did.

17
00:02:03,284 --> 00:02:07,170
Once you've seen that,
the rest of the list reads a certain way.

18
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:18,123
d3d12SDKLayers.dll. Five and a half megabytes.
That's the DirectX 12 debug layer, and it exists to help a developer find bugs.

19
00:02:18,373 --> 00:02:25,733
Three episodes ago Square Enix shipped that exact file.
Same file, same reason: nobody tightened the shipping config.

20
00:02:25,983 --> 00:02:30,600
Then nine Boost libraries.
And one of them is boost python three eleven.

21
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:39,333
Unreal has a Python plugin. It's for the editor: build scripts,
asset pipelines, tools. It has no job in the copy a player downloads.

22
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:43,103
It's here because the plugin was
never switched off before packaging.

23
00:02:43,353 --> 00:02:50,452
And then the two that made me stop. DirectML,
thirteen megabytes. ONNX Runtime, thirteen more.

24
00:02:50,702 --> 00:02:56,860
That's a machine learning inference stack,
in a game whose whole mechanic is spotting a difference between two rooms.

25
00:02:57,110 --> 00:03:07,343
I want that to be something. It probably isn't. Unreal's neural network plugin drags both in,
and this is the second demo I've opened where they're present with nothing to point at.

26
00:03:07,593 --> 00:03:10,721
If you made this and I'm wrong,
the comments are right there.

27
00:03:10,971 --> 00:03:18,017
One more. Seventeen megabytes of Epic Online Services,
in a game the store lists as single player only.

28
00:03:18,267 --> 00:03:23,406
Achievements, telemetry, a plan for later.
A file list can't say which.

29
00:03:23,656 --> 00:03:29,919
Reception. There isn't any. It went up this morning,
no reviews, no player numbers worth reading.

30
00:03:30,169 --> 00:03:39,906
So here's the honest read instead. The idea is proven, the train is a good room to hide things in,
and this build is a first pass that shipped exactly as it left the editor.

31
00:03:40,156 --> 00:03:42,553
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

32
00:03:42,803 --> 00:03:49,327
Unreal, thirty six files, the default executable name,
a debug layer, Python, and an ML runtime.

33
00:03:49,577 --> 00:03:56,885
Worth a dip. It's free, it's a genre that works,
and finding the anomaly in a red velvet carriage is a good twenty minutes.

34
00:03:57,135 --> 00:04:07,786
If you ship in Unreal, take the packaging list from this one. Rename the target,
turn off the Python plugin, and check for SDKLayers. Three checkboxes, ninety megabytes.

35
00:04:08,036 --> 00:04:17,904
That's my read from the outside. If you worked on this and I got something wrong,
say so and I'll pin the correction. Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
